# How to use project rules

Project rules live in **`.cursor/rules/`** as **`.mdc`** files. They give the agent consistent, scoped context.

## Rule file format (.mdc)

Each rule is a markdown file with **YAML frontmatter** at the top:

```yaml
---
description: Short text for "Apply Intelligently" and rule picker
globs: "**/*.py"        # optional — apply when these files are in context
alwaysApply: false      # true = apply to every chat
---
```

Then the rule content (markdown). Cursor supports both `.md` and `.mdc`; use `.mdc` when you want `description` and `globs`.

## When rules are applied

| Type | Frontmatter | When it runs |
|------|-------------|--------------|
| **Always apply** | `alwaysApply: true` | Every chat (e.g. `project-context.mdc`, `architecture.mdc`) |
| **Apply to specific files** | `globs: "tests/**/*.py"` | When files matching the pattern are in context |
| **Apply intelligently** | `description: "..."`, no globs | When the agent decides the rule is relevant from the description |
| **Apply manually** | No globs, `alwaysApply: false` | When you @-mention the rule in chat (e.g. `@how-to-use-rules.mdc`) |

## Rules in this project

| File | Scope | Purpose |
|------|--------|---------|
| `project-context.mdc` | Always | Read docs first, role, critical rules, communication |
| `architecture.mdc` | Always | Layer boundaries (UI → Services → Repos/Parsers → SQLite) |
| `python-style.mdc` | `src/**/*.py` | Code style, dependencies |
| `database.mdc` | `src/clible/db/**`, `**/repositories/*.py` | SQLite, migrations, parameterized SQL |
| `testing.mdc` | `tests/**/*.py` | pytest, fixtures, test layout |
| `git-commits.mdc` | By relevance / manual | Commits, branches |
| `pr-stories.mdc` | By relevance / manual | PR description format (PR_STORY.md, PR body) |
| `how-to-use-rules.mdc` | Manual (@mention) | This file |

## Tips

- To force a rule into context: type `@` in chat and choose the rule.
- Keep rules under ~500 lines; split by topic (as here).
- See [Cursor Rules docs](https://cursor.com/docs/rules) for more.
